<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tamaudun</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1501">1501</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tamaudun is the royal mausoleum of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-sho-dynasty">Second Shō dynasty</a> of Ryukyu at Shuri on Okinawa, built in 1501 by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sho-shin">Shō Shin</a> to receive the remains of his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sho-en">Shō En</a>. Cut into the rock and built of stone in imitation of a palace, it held the remains of later kings and queens. Damaged in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-okinawa">Battle of Okinawa</a> and since restored, it is part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tamaudun.jpg" alt="Tamaudun" width="320" height="203" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: xiquinhosilva · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E7%8E%89%E9%99%B5_Tamaudun_Royal_Mausoleum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Tamaudun is one of three royal mausoleums of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-sho-dynasty">Second Shō dynasty</a> of the Ryukyu Kingdom, beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urasoe">Urasoe</a> yōdore at Urasoe Castle and Izena Tamaudun near Izena Castle. It lies a short distance from Shuri Castle. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sho-shin">Shō Shin</a>, the third king of the line, who reigned from 1477 to 1527, had it built for his father, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sho-en">Shō En</a>. The site covers 2,442 square metres and consists of two stone-walled enclosures and the north-facing tomb, backed by a natural cliff to the south.</p><p>The tomb has three compartments laid out from east to west. Kings and queens lie in the eastern one, princes and other members of the royal family in the western. The central chamber served the Ryukyuan custom of senkotsu: the dead were kept there for a limited time to decompose, after which the bones were washed and placed in urns in the eastern or western chamber according to rank. A stone stele in the outer enclosure records the completion of the work and names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sho-shin">Shō Shin</a> with eight others involved in the building. Stone lions, shisa, guard the tomb, and a courtyard paved with coral fragments lies before it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Seventeen of the 19 kings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-sho-dynasty">Second Shō dynasty</a>, which ruled from 1470 to 1879, are buried here with queens and royal children. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sho-en">Shō En</a> died in 1476, so his remains waited about 25 years before they were placed in the finished tomb. Shō Sen'i was never reburied here, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sho-nei">Shō Nei</a> chose burial at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urasoe">Urasoe</a> yōdore after the Invasion of Ryukyu. The last burials were those of Shō Ten, Prince of Nakagusuku and son of the last king, Shō Tai, in 1920 with traditional royal rites, and of his wife in 1931. Some of the dead remain unidentified.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Standing near the Japanese Supreme Commander's office in 1945, the mausoleum was caught in the artillery bombardment that also struck Shuri Castle. Its east and west chambers were destroyed and the ruins looted, though the tombs and royal remains survived. In 1992 Hiroshi Shō, great-grandson of Shō Tai, gave Tamaudun and the gardens of Shikina-en to the City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naha">Naha</a>. It was named a National Historic Site in 1972, inscribed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> on 2 December 2000 among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gusuku-sites-and-related-properties-of-the-kingdom-of-ryukyu">Gusuku Sites and Related Properties of the Kingdom of Ryukyu</a>, and declared a National Treasure in 2018.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tamaudun” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
